WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.134 --> 00:00:18.330
The next morning Anne realized that Matthew was not looking well.

2
00:00:18.331 --> 00:00:22.661
After he had left, Anne said, "Marilla, is Matthew well?"

3
00:00:22.662 --> 00:00:26.115
"No," said Marilla in a troubled tone.

4
00:00:26.116 --> 00:00:30.180
"His heart has been quite bad this spring, but he keeps working.

5
00:00:30.181 --> 00:00:35.238
Maybe he'll rest now that you're home. You always cheer him up."

6
00:00:35.239 --> 00:00:39.386
Anne looked at Marilla. "You're not looking well yourself, Marilla.

7
00:00:39.387 --> 00:00:42.158
You must rest, now that I'm home."

8
00:00:42.159 --> 00:00:50.468
Marilla smiled affectionately at her girl. "It's not the work, it's my eyes. I'm seeing an optometrist soon."

9
00:00:50.469 --> 00:00:53.897
In the evening Anne went to see Matthew in the fields.

10
00:00:53.898 --> 00:00:56.506
Matthew walked slowly with his head down.

11
00:00:56.507 --> 00:01:00.684
"You've been working too hard, Matthew," she said reproachfully.

12
00:01:00.685 --> 00:01:03.800
"I forget I'm getting older," Matthew replied.

13
00:01:03.801 --> 00:01:08.546
"If I'd been the boy you sent for," said Anne, "I could help you."

14
00:01:08.547 --> 00:01:12.207
"Anne, I'd rather have you than a dozen boys.

15
00:01:12.208 --> 00:01:14.963
It wasn't a boy who got that scholarship.

16
00:01:14.964 --> 00:01:18.850
It was a girl, my girl, whom I'm proud of."

17
00:01:18.851 --> 00:01:22.126
Later that night Anne remembered Matthew's words.

18
00:01:22.127 --> 00:01:28.367
It was the last night before sorrow touched Anne's life, and no life is quite the same afterward.

19
00:01:28.368 --> 00:01:34.264
"Matthew, what is the matter?" It was Marilla who spoke, holding Matthew's head.

20
00:01:34.265 --> 00:01:43.161
Anne burst through the door. "He's fainted," gasped Marilla. "Anne, run for Martin. Quick!"

21
00:01:43.162 --> 00:01:49.922
Martin was the hired man, and he drove to get the doctor, stopping at the Lyndes' house to tell them the news.

22
00:01:49.923 --> 00:01:52.540
Mrs. Lynde came as quickly as she could.

23
00:01:52.541 --> 00:01:57.956
She pushed Anne and Marilla gently aside and then laid her ear on Matthew's heart.

24
00:01:57.957 --> 00:02:00.962
She looked at their anxious faces sorrowfully.

25
00:02:00.963 --> 00:02:07.866
"Oh, Marilla," she said gravely. "I don't think we can do anything for him."

26
00:02:07.867 --> 00:02:13.853
Anne spoke quietly. "Mrs. Lynde, you don't think Matthew is . . . is . . ."

27
00:02:13.854 --> 00:02:19.016
Anne could not say the dreadful word. She turned sick and pale.

28
00:02:19.017 --> 00:02:22.413
The doctor said Matthew's death had been painless.

29
00:02:22.414 --> 00:02:29.023
The news spread quickly through Avonlea, and all day friends and neighbors came to pay their respects.

30
00:02:29.024 --> 00:02:34.153
That night the house was quiet. Matthew's death left a horrible ache.

31
00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:37.447
When Anne sobbed, Marilla came to comfort her.

32
00:02:37.448 --> 00:02:40.813
"What will we do without him?" Anne cried.

33
00:02:40.814 --> 00:02:49.012
"We've got each other, Anne," Marilla said softly. "I don't know what I'd do if you weren't here—if you'd never come."

34
00:02:49.013 --> 00:02:53.066
Slowly life went on, and work was done as before.

35
00:02:53.067 --> 00:02:59.928
Anne, new to grief, thought this was sad—that they could go on in the old way without Matthew.

36
00:02:59.929 --> 00:03:03.591
She felt almost ashamed that she could feel happy again.

37
00:03:03.592 --> 00:03:08.025
She missed him so much, and yet the world still seemed beautiful.

38
00:03:08.026 --> 00:03:15.790
But Mrs. Lynde assured her, "Matthew would want you to be happy, Anne. He liked to hear you laugh."

39
00:03:15.791 --> 00:03:20.144
"I saw Gilbert Blythe today," Marilla said one afternoon.

40
00:03:20.145 --> 00:03:26.142
"What a nice-looking fellow he is," she went on. "He's so tall and manly.

41
00:03:26.143 --> 00:03:29.283
He looks a lot like his father did at the same age.

42
00:03:29.284 --> 00:03:34.111
We used to be very good friends. People called him my boyfriend."

43
00:03:34.112 --> 00:03:38.606
Anne looked up with interest. "Oh Marilla, what happened?"

44
00:03:38.607 --> 00:03:45.873
"We had a fight," Marilla said. "I wouldn't forgive him right away; I wanted to punish him first.

45
00:03:45.874 --> 00:03:50.208
He never came back. The Blythes were very independent.

46
00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:54.208
But I've always wished I'd forgiven him when I had the chance."

47
00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:59.199
"So you've had a bit of romance in your life too," said Anne softly.

48
00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.204
"Yes, I guess. You wouldn't know from looking at me!

49
00:04:03.205 --> 00:04:06.742
But you can never tell people from their outsides."

50
00:04:06.743 --> 00:04:13.107
The next day Anne came home to find Marilla looking dejected. "Are you tired, Marilla?"

51
00:04:13.108 --> 00:04:20.028
"Yes . . . no . . . I don't know," said Marilla wearily. "It's not that."

52
00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:24.335
"Did you see the optometrist?" asked Anne anxiously.

53
00:04:24.336 --> 00:04:33.874
"Yes, I saw him," said Marilla. "He said if I wear glasses and stop reading and sewing, my eyes will be all right.

54
00:04:33.875 --> 00:04:41.509
But if I don't, I'll be blind in six months! Blind! Anne, just think of it."

55
00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:47.228
"Things have changed so much since I came home!" Anne thought sadly.

56
00:04:47.229 --> 00:04:51.769
One afternoon a few days later, Marilla came in from the yard.

57
00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:55.336
She had been talking to a man named Mr. Sadler.

58
00:04:55.337 --> 00:05:05.062
There were tears in Marilla's eyes, and her voice broke as she told Anne, "He heard I was going to sell Green Gables, and he wants to buy it."

59
00:05:05.063 --> 00:05:11.941
"Buy Green Gables?" Anne burst out. "Marilla, are you selling Green Gables?"

60
00:05:11.942 --> 00:05:15.340
"If my eyes were strong, I could stay here.

61
00:05:15.341 --> 00:05:19.381
But I may lose my sight, and I can't stay here alone.

62
00:05:19.382 --> 00:05:23.045
Mrs. Lynde advised me to sell and stay with her.

63
00:05:23.046 --> 00:05:34.917
I'm sorry you won't have a home to come to on vacation . . ." Marilla broke down and wept bitterly.

64
00:05:34.918 --> 00:05:43.118
"You mustn't sell Green Gables," said Anne firmly, "and you won't stay here alone, Marilla. I'll be with you."

65
00:05:43.119 --> 00:05:46.994
Marilla looked at Anne, "What do you mean?"

66
00:05:46.995 --> 00:05:52.204
"I'm not taking the scholarship. I couldn't leave you after all you've done for me.

67
00:05:52.205 --> 00:05:59.701
The Avonlea School is promised to Gilbert Blythe, but I can teach in Carmody. I won't leave you, Marilla."

68
00:05:59.702 --> 00:06:06.066
Marilla was stunned. "But I can't let you sacrifice your education for me."

69
00:06:06.067 --> 00:06:11.670
"Nonsense, there is no sacrifice!" Anne laughed.

70
00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:15.148
"Nothing could be worse than giving up Green Gables.

71
00:06:15.149 --> 00:06:18.819
I'll be a good teacher—and save your eyesight.

72
00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:22.232
Besides, I'll study college courses at home."

73
00:06:22.233 --> 00:06:27.510
"I feel as if you've given me a new life," Marilla said, beaming.

74
00:06:27.511 --> 00:06:36.712
In Avonlea people heard that Anne had given up the idea of going to university and, not knowing about Marilla's eyes, thought she was foolish.

75
00:06:36.713 --> 00:06:41.720
But Mrs. Lynde told Anne, "I'm glad to hear you're not going, Anne.

76
00:06:41.721 --> 00:06:44.825
I don't believe in women going to university."

77
00:06:44.826 --> 00:06:51.473
"But I'll teach at Carmody and study Latin and Greek at home, Mrs. Lynde!" said Anne laughing.

78
00:06:51.474 --> 00:06:56.191
"The school board gave you Avonlea School," Mrs. Lynde said.

79
00:06:56.192 --> 00:07:00.737
Anne jumped to her feet. "I thought they promised it to Gilbert Blythe!"

80
00:07:00.738 --> 00:07:09.669
"They did. But when Gilbert heard you applied, he withdrew his application and suggested you for the job.

81
00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.361
He is going to teach at White Sands.

82
00:07:12.362 --> 00:07:15.195
Wasn't that kind and thoughtful of him?"

83
00:07:15.196 --> 00:07:18.520
"Gilbert did that for me?" Anne murmured.

84
00:07:18.521 --> 00:07:25.125
At dusk Anne went to the little Avonlea graveyard and put fresh flowers on Matthew's grave.

85
00:07:25.126 --> 00:07:28.706
She lingered there, liking the peace of the place.

86
00:07:28.707 --> 00:07:31.617
A tall man came toward her, whistling.

87
00:07:31.618 --> 00:07:37.830
"Gilbert!" Anne said, her cheeks flushed. "Thank you for giving up the school for me."

88
00:07:37.831 --> 00:07:41.678
Gilbert took her hand eagerly. "That's all right, Anne.

89
00:07:41.679 --> 00:07:45.095
Are we going to be friends after this? Have you really forgiven me?"

90
00:07:45.096 --> 00:07:52.409
Anne laughed. "I forgave you the day you rescued me from the brook, although I didn't know it."

91
00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:58.750
When Anne came home later, Marilla asked curiously, "Who was with you, Anne?"

92
00:07:58.751 --> 00:08:03.348
"Gilbert Blythe," answered Anne, blushing. "He walked with me."

93
00:08:03.349 --> 00:08:08.542
"I didn't think you two were such good friends." Marilla smiled.

94
00:08:08.543 --> 00:08:14.406
"We've been good enemies," said Anne. "But it's more sensible to be friends."

95
00:08:14.407 --> 00:08:17.515
Anne sat at her window that night, content.

96
00:08:17.516 --> 00:08:33.122
Green Gables was her home, and the dreams of a little redheaded orphan girl had come true.
